Gabriel, how are you?

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:02.280
Good.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.380
Always a pleasure to be here, Steve.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:05.400
Okay.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.045
Today I have the honor of the pleasure
of talking to my friend Gabriel

00:00:09.045 --> 00:00:14.370
Silva, a fellow Vancouverite, a fellow
polyglot originally from Brazil.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.340
And, uh, we're going to
talk about two subjects.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.779
Uh, one why he's a polyglot, what
he gets out of being a polyglot.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:27.195
And two to touch again on the subject of
that covered briefly with, uh, Leonardo

00:00:27.195 --> 00:00:31.875
of Portugal differences between Brazilian
Portuguese and European Portuguese.

00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:34.965
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:36.465
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:36.915 --> 00:00:40.364
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:43.185
Gabriel, uh, nice to see you.

00:00:43.215 --> 00:00:44.625
I'm in Palm Springs.

00:00:44.745 --> 00:00:46.545
Uh, with my fake bookshelf.

00:00:47.355 --> 00:00:49.040
You're I think in Vancouver.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:49.530
I am.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.920
You are a very accomplished, uh, polyglot.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.760
Uh, I have heard you in a number
of languages, unrelated languages,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.410
Russian, Turkish, you name it.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.210
What motivates you?

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.670
I know what motivates me,
but I want to hear from you.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.720
And I think it'd be, it would
be interesting for my viewers.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.270
What do you get out of being a polyglot?

00:01:11.095 --> 00:01:14.005
Well, I love, I absolutely
love this question.

00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:19.645
And, uh, right now I think that, you know,
the, my motivation goes through phases.

00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:24.325
Uh, initially when I started really
learning and getting into languages, uh,

00:01:24.325 --> 00:01:28.165
it was because it was just individually
attracted to different cultures.

00:01:28.195 --> 00:01:31.255
So, uh, you know, it, it was,
I learned English and it took

00:01:31.255 --> 00:01:36.240
me forever to learn English and
really get to a very fluent level.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.360
And then I was just like, well,
I could, I seemed to really

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.860
suck at learning languages.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.889
So I just want to actually try
to learn French and German.

00:01:44.699 --> 00:01:46.770
Then I started learning
them at the same time.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.809
And I, uh, I had a personal challenge that
I wanted to be, uh, cause I love Paris.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.380
So I just actually, I lived
there for, uh, for a year.

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:56.619
I may go back.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:02.145
I don't know when, and, um, I had this
personal challenge that I wanted to

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:04.725
communicate with Parisians in French.

00:02:05.115 --> 00:02:08.835
And, uh, it also took me forever
to accomplish that, to, to get

00:02:08.835 --> 00:02:12.525
to the point that my French was
decent enough for Parisians not

00:02:12.525 --> 00:02:13.995
to switch to English immediately.

00:02:14.655 --> 00:02:21.075
And, um, and then I was also really into,
I actually dated a German girl, um, and

00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:22.845
then fell in love with the, with Germany.

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:26.790
So I just, I would go there and I
wanted to um, to learn German as

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.760
well, especially because it seemed
like such a difficult language.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.410
So it was also a bit of a challenge.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.340
So then when I, when I managed to learn
those languages and reach fluency, it

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.390
was just like, well, you know, like
I, now I know how to learn language.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:45.075
So I just started going, you know,
getting into different ones and uh,

00:02:45.105 --> 00:02:48.525
because I was attracted to Russian
literature, I thought like, why not

00:02:48.525 --> 00:02:53.015
someday learn Russian so that I can
actually understand those stories?

00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:55.845
And I thought it was going to
take me 20 years to learn Russian,

00:02:55.845 --> 00:02:58.095
but it was quicker than expected.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:02.114
And then I started getting into
Chinese and I started dabbling

00:03:02.114 --> 00:03:03.555
into a ton of languages.

00:03:03.555 --> 00:03:08.774
And then I found my, uh, an even
greater motivation, which is actually

00:03:08.774 --> 00:03:16.095
something that I didn't, uh, originally
suspect would really happen, but it's

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:18.435
just how languages connect people.

00:03:18.614 --> 00:03:24.135
And I, that's something that I, that
fascinates me so much because you know,

00:03:24.135 --> 00:03:28.605
one thing that I absolutely love to
do, um, and this may seem random, but I

00:03:28.605 --> 00:03:32.144
just, like, I always like to, you know,
strike up conversations on the streets.

00:03:32.174 --> 00:03:36.644
Uh, I talk to random people like it was,
uh, I was in London for a little while.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:41.085
Um, after I went, I lived  in Paris and
uh, it was just so much fun to just, you

00:03:41.085 --> 00:03:43.125
know, start, start off talking to people.

00:03:43.125 --> 00:03:48.825
And when you're a polyglot and you speak
these, like these different languages and

00:03:48.825 --> 00:03:52.605
you, you know, you hear like whatever, I
hear some Chinese start speaking Chinese.

00:03:53.185 --> 00:03:57.495
I would hear some, uh, Turkish would
be just like, oh, is that Turkish?

00:03:57.525 --> 00:03:58.545
Oh, you're from Turkey.

00:03:58.815 --> 00:04:01.935
And the barriers just disappear.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.275
It's just absolutely insane.

00:04:04.454 --> 00:04:05.585
How quickly they disappear.

00:04:06.059 --> 00:04:09.420
I want to pick up on some of the things,
some of the themes that you mentioned

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.649
that I think are very important.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.369
First of all, we become a
polyglot one language at a time.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:18.630
Now we may study two languages at the
same time, but we begin by learning one

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.849
other language and then another language.

00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:24.810
So at the beginning on the sort of
journey towards becoming a polyglot,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:26.390
it starts with one language.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.390
For whatever reason, we're
motivated to learn one language.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.610
We're motivated to learn another language.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.590
We realize we know how to do it.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:35.280
We can do it.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.020
And then it kind of snowballs.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.850
That's very much been my experience.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.330
The second thing you're saying
is that languages connect us.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.659
And I think if we look at like, what's
the purpose of life, I think we are

00:04:45.659 --> 00:04:50.400
never happier than when we are connecting
with other people and we can connect

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.695
with other people like in the here
and now or through books and movies,

00:04:55.695 --> 00:05:00.165
and we can connect with people in far
away countries or far away centuries.

00:05:00.165 --> 00:05:04.545
And so the opportunities
to discover human...

00:05:04.605 --> 00:05:08.895
human beings, human activity
is, is so much enhanced by

00:05:08.895 --> 00:05:10.035
learning different languages.

00:05:10.035 --> 00:05:14.115
So the things that you mentioned
are absolutely, I totally agree

00:05:14.115 --> 00:05:15.715
with what you have to say there.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:20.969
Absolutely, and like that I, one
person that comes to mind is,

00:05:20.969 --> 00:05:24.239
uh, Ari, uh, Xioma New York city.

00:05:25.109 --> 00:05:25.289
Right.

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:29.099
And he, because he, uh, and
basically like I do, do some similar,

00:05:29.099 --> 00:05:31.650
like similar videos, like in a
different kind of context really.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.210
Cause I, um...

00:05:33.419 --> 00:05:37.560
and he connects, he goes and he gets,
uh, an amazing reaction from people

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.299
because his Mandarin is so good.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:45.150
And, um, and it's just amazing to
see how like, uh, barriers just like

00:05:45.179 --> 00:05:47.450
absolutely dissolve between people.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:52.275
But It's important in doing that to
make it about genuine communication

00:05:52.275 --> 00:05:54.215
and not simply about performing.

00:05:54.925 --> 00:05:56.405
Absolutely.

00:05:56.535 --> 00:06:00.195
We have to make sure that we genuinely
want to connect with those people.

00:06:00.195 --> 00:06:04.030
We don't want to just use them as a
uh, I'm not saying that Ari does that,

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.650
but I think it's very important to do,
to do this whole thing with respect.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.990
One other thing I should say, uh, you are
a genuine polyglot, genuinely interested

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:16.350
in people, in literature, in so many
different things, but I should point

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:22.890
out that one of your major activities at
your YouTube channel you help Brazilians

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.900
with their English or other languages.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:25.770
I'm not entirely sure.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.110
And, um, you know, Brazil, people,
maybe don't fully realize that

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.840
like it's like the United States,
like it's 200 million people.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.210
It's a huge country, a country
with, again, a mixture of peoples

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.540
from different parts of the world,
including the indigenous people.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.450
It's a fascinating country
with tremendous potential.

00:06:46.229 --> 00:06:50.370
And you're sort of, single-handedly
helping them overcome the obstacles

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.669
that language school has put in front
of them in terms of learning languages.

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:57.299
What are some of your messages
to your followers in, in Brazil?

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:03.359
I would say, you know, um, first one of my
messages we will, for sure, like, listen

00:07:03.359 --> 00:07:05.159
to Steve, like check out his channel.

00:07:05.159 --> 00:07:08.520
He's got so much incredible advice.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:15.120
And second really is, you know, like
what I always say, just like um, take

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.110
language learning into your own hands,
and don't really just depend on the

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:23.130
little course that you bought and
that you're, that you're doing because

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.440
the language learning process is fun.

00:07:25.469 --> 00:07:27.000
You should be engaged in it.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.370
You should, um, get exposure to the
language as much as you can to do things

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.500
that you like, do things that you enjoy.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:40.770
And, um, because a lot of students
really have this passive attitude towards

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.970
learning language, especially in Brazil...

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:44.220
it's changing now.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.520
Not especially Brazil,
everywhere like it's...

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.520
Everywhere, everywhere.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.950
Unless you live in Europe where
the neighboring countries speak

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.060
so many different languages.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:55.420
In Brazil, that's less
motivation to learn I guess.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:56.340
No, no...

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:01.170
but the thing is just like, I think
that, um, it's just because the, a

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:05.070
lot of residents are very, they have
have like a traditional mindset,

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.350
more orthodox way of thinking.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:11.370
And, you know, there's nothing wrong
with that, but just like the mentality

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:14.580
is normally yeah like I'm just going
to go take a course to learn a language

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.520
and rely on that, rely on it and not go
that much further, but it is changing.

00:08:20.679 --> 00:08:24.030
And like you said, yeah, it's,
it is a universal problem.

00:08:24.419 --> 00:08:28.049
Uh, too many, especially, for
example, like in North America, first

00:08:28.049 --> 00:08:31.815
thing that pops up when you say,
oh, Um, I'll pick up Duolingo or

00:08:31.815 --> 00:08:33.555
Rosetta Stone to learn a language.

00:08:33.555 --> 00:08:36.945
And that's just the way
that people think about it.

00:08:36.945 --> 00:08:38.895
Normally like the average person, right.

00:08:38.895 --> 00:08:40.485
If they, when it comes
to learning a language.

00:08:40.485 --> 00:08:41.235
If they go that far.

00:08:41.685 --> 00:08:42.674
If they go that far.

00:08:42.674 --> 00:08:43.245
Exactly.

00:08:44.625 --> 00:08:45.855
Listen, one last thing.

00:08:45.915 --> 00:08:47.505
Uh, we can talk forever.

00:08:47.505 --> 00:08:47.835
We all know.

00:08:48.805 --> 00:08:52.255
Um, so Brazil is this fascinating
country, 200 million people.

00:08:52.255 --> 00:08:55.915
I'm not sure I have the stats right,
but it's around 200 million people.

00:08:56.455 --> 00:08:57.495
Uh, and yet...

00:08:57.495 --> 00:08:59.515
I think it's 210, maybe it's a very big.

00:08:59.574 --> 00:09:00.235
210.

00:09:00.955 --> 00:09:05.425
And Portugal in Europe is a much
smaller country, 10 million people.

00:09:05.425 --> 00:09:09.085
But of course, for Europeans,
it's a much more likely tourist

00:09:09.085 --> 00:09:12.265
destination than Brazil because
Brazil is that much further away.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:16.165
Uh, North Americans may be
more likely to go to Brazil.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.150
But for people who want to learn the
Portuguese language, how important are

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:26.699
the differences between Portuguese in
Europe, in, in, in, uh, Portugal or

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:31.319
for that matter Mozambique or wherever
else Portuguese is spoken versus the

00:09:31.319 --> 00:09:35.189
form of the language or the different
regional forms of the language in Brazil?

00:09:35.219 --> 00:09:39.609
How important are all these
regional varieties of the language?

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:43.200
Uh, I think that a lot of, uh, Portuguese
people may disagree with me, but I

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.040
would say that they're not that relevant
really in the sense that, especially

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.220
for instance, that that is a question
that I get for English all the time,

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.310
too, like, oh, should they learn
British English or should they learn,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.900
uh, American English or Australian
English if I'm going to Australia?

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:02.489
I would say, learn English, learn
the language, um, and then really

00:10:02.489 --> 00:10:07.709
worry about your, your specific,
uh, local accent or dialect later.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:11.640
But of course that, you know, like
you may encounter some minor issues

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.870
if, uh, let's say you learn American
English and then you move to Scotland.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.990
Um, then you know, they have a very...

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.939
That's an extreme example, but yeah.

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:25.709
You're in Glasgow and then like, you're
going to be struggling to understand.

00:10:25.739 --> 00:10:29.985
Then of course you can get a lot of
exposure to content from that area to

00:10:29.985 --> 00:10:32.565
just get acquainted with the accent.

00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:36.015
And the funny thing too is just like,
for instance, just even in the UK

00:10:36.015 --> 00:10:37.965
alone, there's so many regional accents.

00:10:38.045 --> 00:10:42.195
Scouse is so different from, uh,
an, any accent from the south or

00:10:42.195 --> 00:10:44.205
even the Jordie accent in Newcastle.

00:10:44.595 --> 00:10:50.985
But anyway, so the Portuguese basically,
there is just so much, uh, content

00:10:50.985 --> 00:10:53.085
in Brazilian Portuguese, really.

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:57.245
And in Brazil we have so many
also like different accents, uh...

00:10:57.715 --> 00:11:01.275
so like basically, um, slang and
all sorts of different things.

00:11:01.275 --> 00:11:02.865
But I would say learn the language.

00:11:02.925 --> 00:11:07.725
And of course, um, Portuguese in
Portugal is very beautifully spoken.

00:11:07.785 --> 00:11:09.495
It tends to be more formal.

00:11:09.765 --> 00:11:11.175
Uh, of course they have their slang.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:16.890
But, uh, often if you get exposure
to a lot of content from Brazil,

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.490
you may come across a lot of, uh,
local slang and things that are

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.920
going to be used in Brazil, but are
not going to be used in Portugal.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:27.000
And there will be, you know, vocab
that's going to be different as well.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:29.910
I love Portugal, I've been there twice.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:35.430
And, uh, it was just very, very,
like, I'm fascinated, um, about like

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.630
the different little differences,
you know, like sometimes there'd be

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.650
just like a word that's just like,
oh, you guys say that, you know...

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:46.260
Yeah, but, but you're at a very high
level, obviously in all languages, but

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.700
in particular, in your native language.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:52.920
I think the average beginner would
have trouble telling whether this is

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:58.350
Brazilian or Portuguese, wouldn't even
know, um, as is true with Spanish as

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:00.780
is true with all these other languages
that have different regional areas.

00:12:01.725 --> 00:12:03.405
So you can't even hear the difference.

00:12:03.435 --> 00:12:06.315
So beginners, shouldn't worry
about these differences.

00:12:06.465 --> 00:12:07.815
They should just learn words.

00:12:08.175 --> 00:12:10.545
And certainly when I was learning
Portuguese, I listened to

00:12:10.545 --> 00:12:11.805
both Portuguese and Brazilian.

00:12:11.805 --> 00:12:16.005
I want to be able to understand people,
uh, wherever they may come from.

00:12:16.365 --> 00:12:20.505
And so I tend to feel that people
make too much of an issue out of this.

00:12:20.505 --> 00:12:22.365
You know, which variety
are you going to learn?

00:12:22.365 --> 00:12:24.855
Is this Portuguese from
Portugal or is it from Brazil?

00:12:25.275 --> 00:12:27.075
You have to get pretty good.

00:12:27.075 --> 00:12:30.345
You have to be well into intermediate
before you can even hear the difference.

00:12:30.475 --> 00:12:30.785
Yeah.

00:12:31.605 --> 00:12:32.295
Absolutely.

00:12:32.745 --> 00:12:36.255
I think that like, friend, I've heard
some people say like, oh, you know,

00:12:36.435 --> 00:12:41.115
Portuguese from Portugal sounds more
like Russian to a foreign person versus

00:12:41.175 --> 00:12:46.755
Brazilian Portuguese may sound more like
a, I don't know, like some broken form

00:12:46.755 --> 00:12:50.355
of Spanish or whatever, like to whoever
doesn't speak the language or whatever.

00:12:50.714 --> 00:12:54.285
But, um, yeah, I think that, you know,
Steve, you're absolutely right because

00:12:54.285 --> 00:12:58.755
you need to learn the language then if you
just really want to focus on a specific

00:12:58.905 --> 00:13:03.344
area, let's say that you're learning
Portuguese and then you want to move to

00:13:03.435 --> 00:13:10.935
Lisbon or you want to move to Porto or,
uh, versus São Paulo or Bahia, you know,

00:13:10.935 --> 00:13:16.245
you're going to, you can then later worry
about, uh, um, the regional accents, the

00:13:16.245 --> 00:13:19.545
regional like slang way later in the game.

00:13:19.935 --> 00:13:22.785
If you really don't know that
language yet at all, you may think

00:13:22.785 --> 00:13:23.935
Portuguese sounds like Russian.

00:13:24.915 --> 00:13:27.555
Once you know words, you're
more focused on the words.

00:13:27.555 --> 00:13:30.225
And at that point, the
differences become less important.

00:13:30.525 --> 00:13:34.465
If you don't know the language at all,
you would think that Greek and Casti...

00:13:35.085 --> 00:13:37.814
iberian Spanish are the same
language because they have the

00:13:37.814 --> 00:13:39.005
same sounds, the same sounds.

